a='content/os/01-processes/proc-context-switching.md';b=a.replace('.md','.questions.json');src=['https://go.dev/src/runtime/HACKING','https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/kernel-per-CPU-kthreads.html','https://docs.kernel.org/arch/x86/xstate.html','https://docs.kernel.org/arch/x86/pti.html','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/torvalds/linux/master/arch/x86/entry/entry_64.S']
for old,new in [('Lots of voluntary switches means the task spends its life waiting (I/O-bound, or contending on locks). Lots of involuntary ones means it wants more CPU than it gets.','Frequent voluntary switches suggest blocking; frequent involuntary switches suggest preemption. Counts alone do not reveal elapsed time, time spent waiting, or a unique bottleneck.'),('Linux skips the `CR3` load and the TLB stays warm. That\'s why a thread switch is cheaper than a process switch, and one reason threads exist at all.','The scheduler need not change to another process\'s memory map. Translations remain valid, though cache/TLB entries may still be evicted and KPTI may switch page tables on kernel entry/exit. This can avoid work, not guarantee lower total latency.'),('The work above takes roughly **one to a few microseconds** on modern hardware, depending on CPU, kernel version, mitigations and how much SIMD state needs saving. Measure rather than trust a figure.','> [!note] Content gap: timing benchmarks\n> No reproducible benchmark establishes universal context-switch, cache, memory-access or goroutine-switch times here. Those timing claims are omitted. Numerical exercises below use explicit hypothetical costs.'),('    /* 2 switches per round trip */\n    printf("%.0f ns/switch\\n", ns / N / 2);','    /* Includes IPC and syscall overhead. */\n    printf("%.0f ns/half-trip\\n", ns/N/2);'),('Each round trip forces two switches (parent to child, child to parent), plus four syscalls.','Under the same-logical-CPU alternating model, each round trip includes at least the parent-to-child and child-to-parent switches, plus four I/O syscalls. The minimal illustration omits failure/EINTR handling and child reaping.'),('Results of one to a few microseconds per switch are typical.','The measured half-trip includes IPC and wake-up overhead and is not an isolated scheduler instruction cost.'),('A core\'s L1 caches are small (often 32–48 KiB of data), and L2 is typically 1–2 MiB. When task B runs, its code and data push out A\'s lines. When A comes back, its first thousands of memory accesses miss and go to L3 or RAM: roughly 10–40 ns for L3 and around 100 ns for DRAM, versus about 1 ns for L1.','Caches have finite, processor-specific capacities. Another task can evict useful code/data lines, making later accesses require a lower cache level or memory. The number and latency of misses depend on the working sets and memory-level parallelism.'),('If B evicted all of it from L2 and each refill comes from L3 at about 15 ns, A spends around 8,192 × 15 ns ≈ 120 µs re-warming. That\'s far more than the 2 µs switch itself.','Assume every line was evicted, each refill takes a hypothetical 15 ns and refills are fully serialised. The sum is 8,192 × 15 ns = 122.88 µs. This is a simplified latency sum, not a prediction of real elapsed time; overlapping refills and bandwidth matter.'),('If the scheduler moves a task to a different core, *all* its cache state is on the wrong core.','Moving to another core can lose private-cache locality; shared caches, previously cached data and cache-coherence transfers complicate the actual cost.'),('Rough rule of thumb: tens of thousands of switches per second on a busy server is normal. Hundreds of thousands per core, with high system CPU time, deserves a look.','Compare switch rates with workload throughput, CPU time and measured latency. No universal healthy switch-count threshold applies.'),('avoiding switches entirely at the cost of burning 100% of those cores.','reducing scheduling interference while keeping cores busy. Affinity alone does not eliminate interrupts, kernel work or every context switch.'),('so it costs tens to a few hundred nanoseconds.','avoiding kernel-scheduler work on that path; the runtime and workload determine its cost.'),('M:N runtimes must use non-blocking I/O and park threads cleverly.','M:N runtimes may use nonblocking I/O, park user threads or move runnable work to other OS threads around blocking calls.'),('The direct cost is roughly one to a few microseconds; the indirect cost of cold caches and a cold TLB can be much larger.','Direct scheduler work and indirect cache/TLB effects are different costs; both require measurement on the chosen workload.')]:edit(a,old,new,'Remove unsupported universal timing and qualify scheduler/cache mechanisms.',src)
qe(b,'proc-context-switching-q3','explanation','Sharing a memory map avoids changing to another process\'s translations. Validity does not guarantee cache residency, and KPTI entry/exit switches are separate. Total cost depends on task state and workload.','TLB validity is not guaranteed warmth or universally lower cost.',src,option=1)
qe(b,'proc-context-switching-q3','workedExample','Both switches save and restore thread execution state. Threads sharing one memory map avoid a scheduler change to a different address space, while a process switch may require it. PCID can preserve process translations too. Different threads can still have disjoint working sets; cache warmth and total latency are not guaranteed.','Avoid claiming address space is the only possible cost difference.',src)
qe(b,'proc-context-switching-q4','workedExample','The counters show many more preemptions than blocking switches over this task\'s lifetime, consistent with runnable CPU work. They do not reveal wait durations or prove a current bottleneck. Measure counter deltas, CPU utilisation, runnable queues, affinity and workload latency before deciding to add CPU resources.','Lifetime counts do not determine time spent blocked.',src)
qe(b,'proc-context-switching-q6','prompt','In a simplified serial-refill model, a 256 KiB working set is entirely evicted from L2. Assume 64-byte cache lines and a hypothetical 20 ns per L3 refill, with no overlap. What is the sum of refill latencies?','Add serialisation assumption needed for latency multiplication.',src)
qe(b,'proc-context-switching-q6','workedExample','256 × 1,024 / 64 = 4,096 cache lines. With fully serialised hypothetical 20 ns refills, 4,096 × 20 = 81,920 ns = 81.92 microseconds. Real elapsed cost can differ because of prefetch, overlap, bandwidth and which lines are actually accessed.','Do not present sum of latencies as measured elapsed time.',src)
qe(b,'proc-context-switching-q7','explanation','Batching can reduce per-message wake-ups and handoffs. The reduction depends on scheduling, concurrency and existing buffering; two switches per message is not universal.','Remove guaranteed 100-fold reduction.',src,option=1)
qe(b,'proc-context-switching-q8','workedExample','Separate cores add inter-core communication, possible remote wake-ups and idle-state exit costs. A wake-up need not always require a new IPI if the receiving CPU is already running suitable work or polling. Pinning both tasks to one logical CPU removes cross-core placement from the experiment, but the timing still includes IPC, syscalls and other scheduling interference.','Remote wakeup not universally an IPI or dominant cost.',src)
qe(b,'proc-context-switching-q9','workedExample','KPTI uses restricted user page tables and full kernel page tables. User/kernel transitions may switch between them. PCID can retain translations under different tags instead of invalidating non-global entries on every transition; invalidation is still required when mappings change. Actual performance benefit depends on workload and processor, not a fixed count of misses.','Remove unsupported hundreds-of-misses and universal performance effect.',src)
qe(b,'proc-context-switching-q10','explanation','Oversubscription can add scheduler and cache costs. A smaller pool and nonblocking I/O are worth testing; counters and workload measurements must establish the actual bottleneck.','Question symptoms do not prove cache misses dominate.',src,option=1)
qe(b,'proc-context-switching-q10','workedExample','10,000 / 16 = 625 runnable threads per core under the simplified even-distribution assumption. This can cause scheduling contention and disrupt locality, but does not prove every cache is empty on every turn. Compare bounded worker pools, nonblocking I/O, CPU profiles and cache/scheduler counters. The appropriate worker count depends on blocking, CPU work and the service\'s shared data.','Avoid universal cold-cache diagnosis and exact worker-count fix.',src)
qe(b,'proc-context-switching-q11','explanation','The runtime can switch goroutines on an OS thread in user space, avoiding a kernel scheduling operation for that switch. No universal timing is implied.','Remove unsupported nanosecond benchmark.',src,option=0)
save([a,b])
